Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 02:40:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] Give the homepage content a crawler can see

Google's OAuth brand verification rejected the app twice:

    Your homepage is behind a login page.
    The app name 'Datescare' configured for your OAuth consent screen
    does not match the app name on your homepage.

Neither is about the app. Both are about what the served HTML says
before React runs.

index.html shipped an empty <div id="app"></div>. Everything a visitor
reads - the hero, the description, the trust points - is rendered by
React after the bundle downloads. Fetch the URL without executing
JavaScript and the document is meta tags and nothing else: no
description of the service, and the string "Dates.care" appears only
inside <title>. Fetching https://dates.care confirmed it - the response
body carried no content at all. So a checker that does not run JS sees
no homepage, and one that does lands on a screen whose most prominent
controls are Create your free account and Sign in.

The fallback goes inside #app rather than beside it, because
createRoot() clears its container's children on first render. It is
therefore the whole page to anything that does not run the bundle, and
gone the instant React mounts - no flag to unset, no cleanup, no element
left competing with the app. It uses the app's own gradient so the
moment before hydration reads as a first paint.

Welcome.tsx now carries the name in its hero too, which covers a checker
that does render JavaScript. The site previously never displayed its own
name anywhere outside <title>.

Verified by building and loading the result twice: with JavaScript
disabled the page renders an h1 of "Dates.care", 627 characters of
description, and no sign-in control anywhere; with JavaScript on the
fallback is gone and the React hero has replaced it.

Note for whoever sets the consent screen: the app name must be exactly
"Dates.care". "Datescare" is the spelling that was rejected.

